    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "we checked the ink and a new batch actually made it worse. the patches are super random, like they follow no pattern on the fabric. it's almost like the ink is being pulled away or stolen from the paper during the heat cycle. is there any chemical reason why ink would just disappear like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's highly unlikely for the ink to be pulled away or disappear during the heat cycle as the sublimation process should preserve the printed image given that both heat press and inks are properly calibrated for the material. If you're experiencing inconsistencies, it suggests a deeper problem with the sublimation process. Since the ink quality wasn't the issue, there could be an error in your sublimation standardized color bar, improper preparation of the polyester fabric, such as insufficient surface priming or residues, or an obstruction or defect in the sublimation paper causing uneven heat distribution. I would recommend another thorough inspection of the setup:\n\n\n1. Validate your standardized color bar to ensure it is correctly mixed and applied.\n\n2. Rinse or pre-clean the polyester fabric to remove any dust or oils that could interfere with ink adhesion.\n\n3. Inspect the print paper for defects and to confirm it's not hindering the heating process.\n\n4. As a precaution, try running a simple ink-only print to verify the heating element distributes heat evenly across the entire surface of the polyester.\n\n\nIf problems persist after these steps, seek advice from the manufacturer to see if combination factors you're not accounting for could be at play.",
      "source": "target",
